#set text(black, font: "New Computer Modern")

Data flowing from Layer 2 passes through PCS (encoding) → PMA (serialization) → PMD (signal transmission onto the medium). Signal coming from the physical medium reverses this: PMD receives the signal → PMA deserializes it → PCS decodes it back into a clean bitstream for Layer 2.

The three sublayers allow the standard to be modular. A new physical medium (e.g. a new fiber type) only requires changes at the PMD level, the PCS and PMA layers above it remain the same.

===== PMD, Physical Medium Dependence

The PMD is the lowest sublayer and the one closest to the actual physical medium. It handles everything that is specific to the medium itself: generating the signal, amplifying it, and shaping it for transmission. On copper, this means driving voltages. On fiber, it means driving LEDs or lasers. On wireless, Lifi, Free Space Optics, it means generating the radio frequency wave. The PMD is what changes when you change the medium, the sublayers above it (PMA and PCS) can remain the same.

